A||||8||fiero56||electrical gremlins in the parking light system||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||01-29-2001||02:27 PM||||'86SE 2.8 4-speed<P>I've had this problem ever since buying the car, but it's warm enough today to take a look. The amber parking lights in the front fascia do not light up when turned on. They do light up for turn signals though. The lights do work when the hazards are turned on, although the passenger side is much dimmer. If the hazard lights are activated while the parking lights are on, the two lights in the front fascia alternate continuously(driver side on, passenger off, vice versa, like a cop car's headlights do when the overheads are turned on).<BR>Using a test light there is juice at the socket when the parking lights are on, but the bulbs just won't light.<P>I have replaced the hazard relay, parking light relay, and both bulbs. No fix. What's up with this?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||01-29-2001||03:24 PM||||You are exhibiting the classic symptoms of a flakey ground.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||01-29-2001||03:29 PM||||I've been out checking the grounds, and I can't get a ground off the passenger socket. <BR>I can't follow the ground wire, and am not sure where it finally meets the chassis. If I can find the end of the wire, I can find out where/if it has a break in it. Anyone know where the system grounds itself?<P>You are right, Phil.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||01-29-2001||04:07 PM||||just cut the wire and make a new ground. any chassis metal part will do. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||01-29-2001||04:23 PM||||The last one I bought from the east coast (New Jersey) had a bad socket and was doing the same thing. Might be something to check.||||reg||1
Z||000005||LarryB||01-29-2001||04:46 PM||||The 2 sides are isolated so if you had a bad socket (and I have) it would most likely only affect 1 side.  <P>If you have a Helms or Haynes manual you can find the wiring schematic, that and a voltmeter/continuity tester will be a big help.  Troubleshooting the wiring up front is a PITA but doable, the worst part is getting at the harness around where it screws into the firewall. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||01-29-2001||06:17 PM||||I will splice in a temporary new ground and see if it fixes the problem.<P>I checked my Haynes and it doesn't show the location where the ground meets the chassis, that's why I checked here. But if both sockets have separate grounds, it shouldn't matter anyway. I'll try it out.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||01-30-2001||02:03 PM||||I think the ground is located up near the headlight buckets.  If I remember right, you have to remove the headlight assy to get to it.<P>You should see the ground wire on the outboard face/surface.  This is were it is on my '84, chances are it's the same for '86's.<P>It's real easy to get corrosion buildup between the little sheet metal screw and the wire.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||01-30-2001||03:18 PM||||I got it finxed. Turns out the little metal strip that grounds the base of the bulb had fallen out, and I hadn't noticed. I wired up a new grounding setup for that socket, and the parking lights are working. Also fixed the marker lights up front too.<P>I also got the headlights working too. Had a bad relay, and the wiring was hacked up real bad from the first owner installing/removing a very poorly wired alarm. The passenger motor is very slow and making some noises, I'll have to open it up, I just rebuilt both motors.||||reg||1
